### Summary

#### Concepts

- **Grouping** (`group_by()`) a data frame allows us to perform calculations separately for different groups of observations.
- **Summary statistics** (`summarise()`) can be calculated for each group or the whole data frame to produce a new summary data frame.
- **Joins** combine information from multiple data frames based on one or more shared columns.
- Different types of join determine which rows are retained when values are not present in both data frames.
- Data can be stored in **wide format**, with multiple measurements in different columns, or **long format**, with measurements spread across multiple rows.
- The `%in%` operator tests whether values occur within a set of values, which is useful when filtering on several categories.

#### Functions

| Function | What it does | Level |
|---|---|---|
| `group_by()` | Groups rows of a data frame according to the values in one or more columns. | Core |
| `summarise()` | Calculates summary values, either for a whole data frame or for groups in a grouped data frame. | Core |
| `max()` | Returns the largest value from a set of values. | Core |
| `min()` | Returns the smallest value from a set of values. | Core |
| `median()` | Calculates the median of a set of values. | Core |
| `full_join()` | Joins two data frames while retaining all rows from both. | Core |
| `inner_join()` | Joins two data frames while retaining only rows shared by both. | Core |
| `left_join()` | Joins two data frames while retaining all rows from the first data frame. | Core |
| `right_join()` | Joins two data frames while retaining all rows from the second data frame. | Core |
| `write_csv()` | Saves a data frame to a CSV file. | Core |
| `pivot_longer()` | Reshapes a data frame from wide to long format. | Recommended |
| `ends_with()` | Selects columns whose names end with specified text. | Recommended |
| `across()` | Applies the same operation to multiple columns. | Extension |
| `pivot_wider()` | Reshapes a data frame from long to wide format. | Extension |

